.TH DSTART 8 "2026-10-26"
.SH NAME
dstart \- open the news port and start diablo or dreaderd as news
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dstart
.B diablo
|
.B dreaderd
[
.I options
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I dstart
opens the listening socket (by default the nntp port on all IPv4
addresses), switches to the user and group news, and runs diablo or
dreaderd from path_home/dbin with the open socket passed as
.BI \-b fd
and the remaining options.
.PP
It reads these options itself and also passes them on:
.TP
.BI \-B " host" [: port ]
Bind to this host (and port).
.TP
.BI \-P " " [ host :] port
Bind to this port (and host).
.TP
.BI \-R " size" " \fR,\fP \-T " size
Set the receive and send buffer sizes of the socket.
.TP
.BI \-C " diablo.config"
Use this diablo.config.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Only one IPv4 address can be bound; start dreaderd as root to listen on
several addresses or on IPv6.
.I diablo-ctl
(adm/diablo-ctl) is the usual way to start the daemons.
.SH SECURITY
Don't install
.I dstart
setuid root.  It honours
.B \-C
and DIABLO_CONFIG_PATH, which decide the program it runs, so a setuid
.I dstart
lets any local user run programs as news.  Run it as root instead.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
diablo(8),
dreaderd(8)
